        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Marathon</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">12 September 490 BCE in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a>, the conventional date calculated by August Böckh in 1855; if the Spartan calendar was a month ahead of the Athenian, 12 August 490 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">The plain and bay of Marathon, about 27 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> under ten generals, with the war archon Callimachus, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a> as the general most experienced against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, with 1,000 Plataeans; ancient writers give 9,000 or 10,000 Athenians. The Persians under Datis and Artaphernes, for whom modern estimates run from 20,000 to 100,000 infantry, with a consensus of perhaps 25,000, and about 1,000 cavalry</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Athenian and Plataean wings broke the Persian wings and then turned on the centre; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> fled to their ships and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> counted 6,400 Persian dead and 192 Athenian.</span></div>
        </div>
