    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Athenians fought without the Spartans, who had sent word that a religious festival kept them at home, and the Spartan army arrived only the day after the battle.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> gives no figure for the Athenian army and none for the Persian; ancient writers' numbers for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> range from 200,000 to 600,000, and modern historians put the infantry at perhaps 25,000.</li><li>The story of a runner carrying the news from Marathon to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> first appears centuries after the battle; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>'s runner went from Athens to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> before it.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The modern marathon race comes from the legend of a runner from Marathon to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, which is not in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>; the idea was proposed by Michel Bréal for the first modern Olympic Games in 1896.</p></aside>
    </section>

