    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">499 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Ionian cities revolt against Persian rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria send a force that burns the lower city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">494 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Persian naval victory at Lade all but ends the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">490 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Datis and Artaphernes cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, subdue the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> and besiege and burn Eretria</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">490 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> land at Marathon and the Athenians block the two exits from the plain</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">490 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">After about five days of stalemate the Athenians attack and break the Persian wings, then the centre</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">490 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Persian fleet turns back from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> after the Athenian army returns by forced march</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">486 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Darius's Egyptian subjects revolt, postponing a new expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">480 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> begins the second Persian invasion of Greece</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-marathon" data-v="eb54fe68af" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies form their lines</b> The two tribes in the Greek centre were drawn up four ranks deep and the tribes on their flanks eight deep. One explanation is that this made the Athenian line as long as the Persian line.</li><li><b>The Greeks advance and the lines meet</b> On a signal from Miltiades the Athenians advanced and probably broke into a run at the limit of the archers' effectiveness. They went through the Persian arrows, protected for the most part by their armour, and met the enemy line.</li><li><b>The Greek centre is pushed back</b> The fighting lasted a long time. In the centre the Persians and Sacae prevailed and broke through in pursuit inland, while on each wing the Athenians and Plataeans prevailed.</li><li><b>The Persian wings collapse and the Greek wings turn in</b> The Athenian wings quickly routed the Persian levies on the flanks. They then turned inwards to surround the Persian centre, which had been more successful against the thin Greek centre.</li><li><b>The Persian centre breaks and flees to the ships</b> The Persian centre broke in panic towards its ships, pursued by the Greeks. Some, unaware of the local terrain, ran towards the swamps, where unknown numbers drowned.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/darius-i.jpg" alt="Darius I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Darius_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rumlu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first Persian invasion of Greece grew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a>. In 499 BCE the Ionian cities rose against Persian rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria sent a force that marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> and burned the lower city before Persian horsemen drove it back to the coast with many losses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> vowed to punish both cities; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> he shot an arrow upwards and asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> for vengeance on the Athenians, and had a servant remind him of them three times before dinner each day. The Persian victory at Lade in 494 BCE all but ended the revolt.</p><p>Athens had its own reasons for enmity with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. After the tyrant Hippias was expelled in 510 BCE, he fled to the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> Artaphernes at Sardis and promised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> control of Athens if they restored him. The Athenians, who had reformed their state under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a>, would not take him back, and went to open war with Persia. Darius meanwhile was extending his empire into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, having subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><p>In 492 BCE Darius sent his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardonius">Mardonius</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, but a storm wrecked the fleet. In 490 BCE he sent a naval expedition under Artaphernes, the satrap's son, and Datis, a Median admiral, to bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> into the empire and then force Eretria and Athens to submit or be destroyed. After taking Naxos the force reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> in mid-summer, besieged and burned Eretria, and sailed south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> on Hippias's advice.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Marathon&#8221; cites where it covers Background:</p><ul><li>Joseph Roisman, A Companion to Ancient Macedonia (John Wiley and Sons, 2011)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hoplite"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hoplite.jpg" alt="Hoplite" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">Hoplite</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spartan_hoplite-1_from_Vinkhuijzen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vinkhuijzen collection, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> gives no figure for the Athenian army. Cornelius Nepos, Pausanias and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> give 9,000 Athenians and 1,000 Plataeans, and Justin 10,000 Athenians and 1,000 Plataeans; these figures are close to those Herodotus gives for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> eleven years later, and modern historians generally accept them as reasonable. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> then had a population of about 315,000 including slaves, so the army was about 3 per cent of it. There were ten generals, one elected by each tribe, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a>, and the war archon Callimachus was in overall command.</p><p>Herodotus says the fleet had 600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a> but does not estimate the army, describing only a large, well packed infantry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides</a>, a near contemporary, gives 200,000 for the expedition. Later writers give 200,000 infantry and 10,000 cavalry (Nepos, who says only 100,000 fought), 300,000 (Plutarch, Pausanias and the Suda), 500,000 (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysias">Lysias</a>) and 600,000 (Justin). Modern historians propose from 20,000 to 100,000 infantry, with a consensus of perhaps 25,000, and about 1,000 cavalry. Herodotus names the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and the Sakae at the centre of the line.</p><p>The Athenians held a strong position on a spur of Mount Agrieliki beside the plain, and blocked both exits. They were probably outnumbered at least two to one, had stripped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> of defenders, and could not afford defeat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">Hoplites</a> were vulnerable to cavalry, and the Persian force contained many missile troops, lightly armoured and no match for hoplites in melee. Time favoured the Athenians, because every day brought the Spartans nearer.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Marathon&#8221; cites where it covers Persians:</p><ul><li>Nick Sekunda, Marathon, 490 BC: The First Persian Invasion of Greece (Praeger, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/miltiades"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miltiades.jpg" alt="Miltiades" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miltiades_(Roman_replica).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The armies faced each other for about five days. Pheidippides, sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> for help, found the Spartans at the festival of Carneia and was told that they could not march until the full moon. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> attacked before the Spartans came. One theory is that the Persian cavalry had left, perhaps being re-embarked to sail round Cape Sounion against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and that the Athenians moved to exploit it; the other is that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> advanced and the Athenians then took the offensive. Neither can be firmly established; both may be true.</p><p>The armies stood about 1,500 metres apart. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a> ordered the Leontis tribe under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> and the Antiochis tribe under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristides">Aristides</a> to form the centre only four ranks deep, and the other tribes on the flanks eight deep. Some modern commentators see a plan for envelopment; others think the Greeks lacked the training for it and thinned the centre so that their line would match the Persian line in length. The Athenians advanced, probably walking until they reached the range of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a>, about 200 metres, and then running through the arrows; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says the Persians thought this madness.</p><p>Herodotus says the Persians and Sakae in the centre prevailed and pursued the Greeks inland, but on each wing the Athenians and Plataeans won. They let the routed wing troops flee and brought the wings together against the men who had broken through the centre. The Persians broke towards their ships, and some ran into the swamps, where an unknown number drowned. The Athenians followed them to the shore and captured seven ships, though most got away.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xerxes-i.jpg" alt="Xerxes I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Museum_of_Iran_Darafsh_(785).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Darafsh, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> records that 6,400 Persian bodies were counted on the field, and it is unknown how many more died in the swamps. He reports that the Athenians lost 192 men, among them the war archon Callimachus and the general Stesilaos; he does not say how many Plataeans were killed. The dead of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> were buried on the battlefield in two mounds, and an epigram by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simonides-of-ceos">Simonides</a> was written on the Athenian tomb.</p><p>Herodotus says the Persian fleet then sailed round Cape Sounion to attack Athens directly, while some modern historians place that attempt just before the battle. Either way, the Athenian army marched back about 40 kilometres at great speed and arrived, he says, late in the afternoon, in time to prevent a landing, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> turned back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The two tribes of the centre stayed to guard the field under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristides">Aristides</a>. Herodotus also records a rumour that the move had been arranged with the Alcmaeonid family and that a shield signal was given; it cannot be told whether this was true.</p><p>The Spartan army arrived the next day, having covered 220 kilometres in three days, toured the battlefield and agreed that the Athenians had won a great victory. Darius began raising a larger army, but in 486 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> revolted and he died while preparing to march there. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> put down the revolt and restarted preparations; the second invasion began in 480 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The main source for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a> is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, born in 484 BCE at Halicarnassus, then under Persian rule, who wrote his Histories around 440 to 430 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> began his own history where Herodotus stopped, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> accused Herodotus in an essay of being a lover of barbarians. His reputation has been rehabilitated since the 19th century by archaeological finds, although the prevailing view is that his troop numbers and dates should be treated with scepticism, and some historians think he invented much of his story. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, Plutarch, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> and others add detail, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpent-column">Serpent Column</a> supports some of Herodotus's claims.</p><p>No source gives the course of the battle in detail, and the surviving account is Greek. Historians dispute why the battle began when it did: Herodotus's story that the generals commanded in rotation and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a> waited for his own day is called problematic, since there is little evidence for such a rotation. They also dispute the dates, since the lunisolar calendar dates of Herodotus depend on when the Spartans held their festival, and the conventional 12 September could be a month early.</p><p>The numbers are the most doubtful part. The ancient Persian figures run to many times the modern ones. Whether the Athenians planned an envelopment, and whether the Persian cavalry was present at all, are also disputed, as is the cause of the Greek success: most scholars point to better equipment and tactics, while Lazenby thought it rested on the courage of ordinary amateur soldiers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aeschylus.jpg" alt="Aeschylus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herma_of_Aeschylus,_Klas08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For the Persian empire the defeat barely touched its vast resources, but for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> it was the first time they had beaten the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>. It showed that resistance was possible, that the Persians could be beaten, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> could win without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, which was regarded as the major military power. Militarily, it was the first time a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplite</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> faced mainly lightly armed troops, and it revealed how effective hoplites could be, although the phalanx remained vulnerable to cavalry.</p><p>In later centuries the battle came to be seen as a defining moment for the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athenian-democracy">Athenian democracy</a> and the start of a golden age. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a> held that Marathon was more important, even as an event in British history, than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeschylus">Aeschylus</a>, who fought there, is said to have thought it his greatest achievement, for his gravestone names Marathon and not his plays.</p><p>Legends gathered round it: Pan, who was said to have frightened the Persians, the phantom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a>, a rustic with a plough who was honoured as a hero, and a vow to sacrifice as many goats as there were Persians slain, so that 500 were offered yearly. The runner who dies after announcing the victory is first told by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> in the 1st century CE, citing Heracleides of Pontus. Michel Bréal proposed a marathon race for the first modern Olympics in 1896, and its distance was fixed at 42.195 kilometres.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Marathon in 490 BCE the Athenians and Plataeans attacked a Persian force sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>, broke its wings and drove it to the ships. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> counted 6,400 Persian and 192 Athenian dead. The first Persian invasion of Greece ended, and a second came in 480 BCE.</em></p>

